,"url":"https://www.dawn.com/news/1195875"}]},"2018":{"date":"25 July 2018","polled":"270 of 272","postponed":"2 seats did not poll","turnout":"52%","cands":"3,431","tight":"79","summary":"Nawaz Sharif was disqualified from office for life in April and jailed twelve days before the poll. Some 370,000 troops were deployed, many stationed inside polling stations and given magisterial powers. On election night the Election Commission's new Result Transmission System collapsed and results ran days late. Observers drew a sharp line between the campaign, which they criticised heavily, and the conduct of voting itself, which they largely praised — while faulting the count.","quotes":[{"who":"EU Election Observation Mission","when":"Final report, October 2018","q":"There was no level playing field for electoral contestants","url":"https://www.eods.eu/library/final_report_pakistan_2018_english.pdf"},{"who":"EU Election Observation Mission","when":"On polling day itself","q":"Voting was assessed as well-conducted and transparent","url":"https://www.eods.eu/library/final_report_pakistan_2018_english.pdf"},{"who":"Human Rights Commission of Pakistan","when":"Pre-poll statement, 16 July 2018","q":"blatant, aggressive and unabashed attempts to manipulate the outcome","url":"https://hrcp-web.org/hrcpweb/attempts-to-maneuver-polls-unacceptable-hrcp/"}]},"2024":{"date":"8 February 2024","polled":"265 of 266","postponed":"NA-8 Bajaur postponed","turnout":"48%","cands":"5,112","tight":"68","summary":"The Supreme Court stripped PTI of its bat symbol on 13 January; its candidates contested as independents under individual symbols, which is how the returns record them. Polling in NA-8 Bajaur was called off after a candidate was shot dead. Mobile networks were shut down nationwide on polling day, results came roughly fifteen hours past the legal deadline, and the gap between polling-station Form 45s and consolidated Form 47s became the central dispute. The EU sent only a small expert mission, whose report has never been published.","quotes":[{"who":"European Union","when":"High Representative, 9 February 2024","q":"We regret the lack of a level playing field","url":"https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2024/02/09/pakistan-statement-by-the-high-representative-on-behalf-of-the-european-union-on-the-general-elections/"},{"who":"Commonwealth Observer Group","when":"Final report, published September 2025","q":"impinged on the credibility, transparency and inclusiveness of the electoral process","url":"https://thecommonwealth.org/news/cog-releases-final-report-pakistan-2024-general-elections"},{"who":"Human Rights Commission of Pakistan","when":"Election report, February 2024","q":"The integrity of the 2024 elections was compromised","url":"https://www.thenews.com.pk/print/1158869-hrcp-releases-2024-election-report-demands-independent-audit"}]},"2002":{"date":"10 October 2002","polled":"270 of 272","postponed":"NA-262 and NA-263 re-polled in November","turnout":"42%","cands":"2,049","tight":"59","summary":"Parliament had been dissolved after the coup of 12 October 1999; the Supreme Court validated the takeover but required elections within three years. Pervez Musharraf took a five-year presidential term through a referendum in April 2002, then issued the Legal Framework Order in August, amending the constitution before the poll. Candidates were required to hold a bachelor's degree, with madrasa certificates treated as equivalent, and anyone who had twice been prime minister was barred; Benazir Bhutto and Nawaz Sharif were both abroad and did not contest. Separate electorates for religious minorities were abolished and the voting age was lowered from 21 to 18. Polling in NA-262 and NA-263 was re-run in November.","quotes":[{"who":"EU Election Observation Mission","when":"Final report, 2002","q":"resulted in serious flaws being inflicted on the electoral process","url":"https://www.eods.eu/library/FR%20PAKISTAN%202002_en.pdf"},{"who":"EU Election Observation Mission","when":"On polling day itself","q":"polling day itself had gone relatively smoothly","url":"https://www.eods.eu/library/FR%20PAKISTAN%202002_en.pdf"},{"who":"National Democratic Institute","when":"Pre-election delegation, 9 September 2002","q":"provides for a very limited transfer of power to elected civilian representatives","url":"https://ndi.org/sites/default/files/1434_pk_election_090902_5.pdf"}]},"1993":{"date":"6 October 1993","polled":"202 of 207","postponed":"5 seats filled at later by-elections","turnout":"41%","cands":"1,405","tight":"55","summary":"President Ghulam Ishaq Khan dismissed Nawaz Sharif's government in April 1993 under Article 58(2)(b); the Supreme Court reinstated it, and in July the army brokered the resignation of both men. Moeen Qureshi, a former World Bank official with no domestic base, led the caretaker government, which published lists of loan defaulters and tax delinquents and required candidates to declare their own. Around 150,000 troops were deployed and judicial officers replaced civil servants as presiding officers. The MQM boycotted the National Assembly poll while contesting the provincial assemblies, and turnout in Karachi fell to roughly 15 per cent. No party won a majority; Benazir Bhutto formed a coalition on 19 October. A Commonwealth group and an NDI delegation both observed, but neither report is available online.","quotes":[{"who":"Human Rights Watch","when":"World Report 1994","q":"the low voter turnout of 40 percent, and 15 percent in Karachi where a local party enforced a boycott","url":"https://www.refworld.org/reference/annualreport/hrw/1994/en/35752"},{"who":"Dawn","when":"Retrospective, 13 April 2013","q":"The army became the de facto ruler of the country and dominated the administration of the elections.","url":"https://www.dawn.com/news/802238"}]},"1997":{"date":"3 February 1997","polled":"204 of 207","postponed":"3 seats polled later","turnout":"36%","cands":"1,747","tight":"19","summary":"President Farooq Leghari dismissed Benazir Bhutto's government in November 1996 under Article 58(2)(b), citing corruption, extrajudicial killings and interference with the judiciary; the Supreme Court upheld the dissolution in January. Malik Meraj Khalid led the caretaker administration. Turnout was the lowest in any Pakistani general election to that point. PML-N won 135 of the seats polled on the day, close to a two-thirds majority, and Bhutto alleged that officials had inserted extra ballots. Polling in parts of Karachi was disrupted by violence. A Commonwealth observer group attended; its report is not available online.","quotes":[{"who":"International Progress Organization observer team","when":"To the UN Commission on Human Rights, 1997 — findings focused on Karachi","q":"A condition of lawlessness persisted during the whole electoral campaign and on the election day itself.","url":"https://i-p-o.org/pakelect.htm"}]},"1990":{"date":"24 October 1990","polled":"207 of 207","postponed":"4 seats returned unopposed","turnout":"45%","cands":"1,226","tight":"37","summary":"President Ghulam Ishaq Khan dismissed Benazir Bhutto's government on 6 August 1990 under Article 58(2)(b), and appointed Ghulam Mustafa Jatoi — an opposition leader — as caretaker prime minister. The PPP contested inside the People's Democratic Alliance against the Islami Jamhoori Ittehad, and rejected the result as rigged. Twenty-two years later the Supreme Court, deciding the Asghar Khan case, found that an election cell in the Presidency had channelled intelligence funds to favoured candidates against the PPP. Registered electorate and turnout are not recorded per seat in the available returns; four seats were returned unopposed.","quotes":[{"who":"Supreme Court of Pakistan","when":"Asghar Khan case, 19 October 2012","q":"The general election held in the year 1990 was subjected to corruption and corrupt practices.","url":"https://www.dawn.com/news/757862"},{"who":"Supreme Court of Paki